          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            Broadly speaking
            %em nondeterministic
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computations"} computations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algorithms"} algorithms
            are those whose outcome is
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#RelationToPotentiality"} indefinite
          %p
            Beware that in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexity+theory"} complexity theory
            %em shortest path
            that a non-deterministic machine
            %em could
            potentially take to arrive at a desired result (cf. the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#NIST"} NIST entry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexity+class"} complexity class
            “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NP"}> NP
            ” .
          %p (…)
          %h2#formalizations Formalizations
          %h3#FormalizationViaIndefinitenessEffects Via indefiniteness-effects
          %p
            A basic
            = succeed "-model" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/program"} program
            on an unspecified parameter
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \colon B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observation"} observing
            the actual value of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+variable"} random variable
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            and/or by making a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurement"} measurement
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+process"} stochastic process
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ExamplesQuantumComputation"} below
          %p
            In this model, a non-deterministic program taking input
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data"} data
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+type"} data type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and producing output data of
            %em nominal
            type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D'
            would be an actual (determinstic) program of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi prog
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi D
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  prog \;\colon\; D \longrightarrow \big( B \to D' \big)
                  \\,,
          %p
            whose
            %em de facto
            output
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+type"} data type
            is not
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D'
            but the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to D'
            = succeed "-result" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi prog
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prog(d)
            for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \colon D
            remains “un-determined” or “indefinite” unless and until a parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \colon B
            is somehow chosen (say “randomly”, “experimentally” or in whichever way), in which case only we obtain definite data
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi prog
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prog(d)(b) \;\colon\; D'
          %p It makes sense to say that the data type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi B
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bigcirc_B D' \;\coloneqq\; \big( B \to D'\big)
          %p
            exhibits a “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            \-indefinitess effect” that goes along with the execution of the program, making its output indefinite. Such
            %em side effects
            in computing may be modeled by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
            (see
            %a{:href => "monad+in+computer+science#BasicIdea"} there
            for more). Indeed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_B
            is a classical example of such a monad, often called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
          %blockquote
            %p
              Incidentally, in terms of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
              the parameter type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              plays the role of the set of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds"}> possible worlds
              ” in which the given non-deterministic computation may take definite values. For more on this modal logic perspective see at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity+and+possibility"} necessity and possibility
              the section
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#RelationToPotentiality"} relation to potentiality
          %p
            Now the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_B
            allows to
            %em carry along
            this
            %em indefiniteness effect
            so that followup programs, nominally waiting for input data in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D'
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \colon B
          %p Concretely, if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi prog
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi B
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  prog \;\colon\; D \to \bigcirc_B D'
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi prog
                  %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi B
                  %mi D
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  prog' \;\colon\; D' \to \bigcirc_B D''
          %p
            are two such non-deterministic programs but conditioned on the
            %em same
            global random variable
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \colon B
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+composition"} Kleisli composition
            (which involves the categorical
            = succeed "-operation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplication"} multiplication
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi prog
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext &gt;&gt;=
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi prog
                  %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≡
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi bind
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ◯
                        %mi B
                  %mi prog
                  %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∘
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi prog
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi B
                  %mi D
                  %mo ″
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  prog
                  \\;\;\text{&gt;&gt;=}\;\;
                  prog'
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  \\equiv
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  bind^{\bigcirc_B} prog' \;\circ\; prog
                  \\;\colon\;
                  D \to \bigcirc_B D''
                  \\,.
          %p
            Of course, in general the non-determinism of a program need not be parameterized over a fixed parameter type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B_i)_{i \colon I}
            of parameters is known beforehand, one can form their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+type"} product type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ≡
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \equiv \prod_i B_i
            and proceed as above, but in general even that sequence may not be pre-specified and further effect handling besides a fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
            needs to be considered (…).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#ExamplesQuantumComputation Quantum computation
          %p
            A fundamental example of non-deterministic computation is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            produces outcomes that – to the best knowledge of the current state of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+physics"} fundamental physics
            – are fundamentally non-deterministic in that there is not even in principle a way to predict a given outcome with certainty (only their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+distribution"} probability distribution
            is fixed by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
          %p
            The possible results of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            are indexed by a “measurement basis” which is exactly a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"}> finite
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            serving as the parameter type in the
            = succeed "-discussion" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FormalizationViaIndefinitenessEffects"} above
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+types"} linear types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
            here is a linear version of the classical reader monad – see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+reader+monad"} quantum reader monad
            for details.
          %p
            A formulation of (non-deterministic)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            in terms of the resulting
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            is discussed at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits+via+dependent+linear+types"} quantum circuits via dependent linear types
          %p
            But in fact the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+reader+monad"} quantum reader monad
            is (for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monad"} Frobenius monad
            and as such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "quantum+reader+monad#QuantumReaderMonadIsSpecialFrobeniusWriterMonad"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
            (
            %a{:href => "quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories#CoeckePavlović08"}> Coecke &amp; Pavlović (2008)
            )
          %h3#ExamplesDifferentialLinearLogic Differential linear logic
          %p
            Something reminiscent of – but different from – non-deterministic computation appears (see the quotes
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#QuotesForDifferentialLinearLogic"} below
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+linear+logic"} differential linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut-elimination"} cut-elimination
            procedure has been argued to “intuitively correspond” to non-determinism:
          %p
            The differential linear cut rule receives as input a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            and returns as output a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+sum"} formal sum
            of proofs of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+sum"} formal sum
            of proofs. The nondetermism is here realized by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/addition"} addition
            of proofs as formal sums of proofs that can be understood as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
            but in not exactly the same way that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+disjunction"} linear disjunction
            because it’s a disjunction of proofs and not of formulas. An algorithm which returns
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{1} + \pi_{2}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{1},\pi_{2}
            are two proofs of the same type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            returns in fact the disjunction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{1}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{2}
            because it can’t chose between the two possibilities. On the level of the syntactic proof theory, one cannot code this sum only by the intermediate of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproduct"} biproduct
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+category"} semiadditive category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cuts"} cuts
            that will not be eliminated: in a semiadditive category, the sum is defined by combining the structural morphisms of the products and coproducts, which are then equal, through composition, ie. through the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut+rule"} cut rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CMon-enriched+symmetric+monoidal+category"} CMon-enriched symmetric monoidal category
            where we have access to a sum of morphism without necessarily biproducts. In the case where one considers the additive connectors in the logic, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+disjunction"} additive disjunction
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+conjunction"} additive conjunction
            are then equated under the presence of formal sums and one can reobtain this formal sum through the biproduct in the semantics. But the semantics is not able to explain the syntactic subtleties related to cut elimination and the difference between a formal sum of two proofs and a sum obtained through cuts and biproducts.
          %p
            In contradistinction, the cut elimination of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            is a deterministic algorithm and doesn’t imply formal sums of proofs. Thus, in linear logic, if one includes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+disjunction"} additive disjunction
            and the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+conjunction"} additive conjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproduct"} biproduct
            in the syntax, as in differential linear logic.
          %p#QuotesForDifferentialLinearLogic
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Ehrhard08"} Ehrhard (2008), Introduction
          %blockquote
            %p “when one linearizes a proof obtained by contracting two linear inputs of a proof, one has to choose between these two inputs, and there is no canonical way of doing so: we take the non-deterministic superposition of the two possibilities. Syntactically, this means that one must accept the possibility of adding proofs of the same formula, which is standard in mathematics, but hard to accept as a primitive logical operation on proofs.”
          %p
            However,
            %a{:href => "differential+linear+logic#EhrhardtRegnier09"} Erhardt &amp; Regnier (2009), p. 3
            admit that:
          %blockquote
            %p
              “Notice that in our axiomatization of sums in the differential lambda-calculus, the non-deterministic reduction rule
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo +
                    %mi s
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s + s' \to s
              will not be valid, but sums will nevertheless intuitively correspond to a version of non-deterministic choice where all the actual choice operations are postponed: the result of a term reduction will in general be a large formal sum of terms, and we can reduce the really ”non-deterministic“ part of the computation to a unique ultimate step consisting in choosing one term of that sum. This step is however a fiction and will not appear as a reduction rule of our calculus”
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"}> computation
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-deterministic+automaton"} non-deterministic automaton
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/repeat-until-success+computing"} repeat-until-success computing
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reversible+computation"} reversible computation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+computing"} parallel computing
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Sipser2012
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Sipser"}> Michael Sipser
              , §1.2 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to the Theory Of Computation
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.mog.dog/files/SP2019/Sipser_Introduction.to.the.Theory.of.Computation.3E.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://fuuu.be/polytech/INFOF408/Introduction-To-The-Theory-Of-Computation-Michael-Sipser.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nondeterministic_algorithm"} Nondeterministic algorithm
          %p Nondeterministic Turing machines:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Sipser2012"} Sipser (2012), §3.2
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nondeterministic_Turing_machine"} Nondeterministic Turing machine
          %p
            The notion in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexity+theory"} complexity theory
          %ul
            %li#NIST
              NIST,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/nondetermAlgo.html"} nondeterministic algorithm
          %p
            The above treatment of non-deterministic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+reader+monad"} quantum reader monad
            is made explicit in
          %ul
